    "body": "Roche is offering $400 million upfront and over $15 billion in biobucks to team up with Hansoh Pharmaceutical in hopes of developing a portfolio of new antibody-drug conjugates using the latter’s in-house tech.\nRoche is returning to China biotech partner Hansoh Pharmaceutical, expanding upon an antibody-drug conjugate alliance with a new, wide-ranging deal that could top $15 billion.\nRoche has inked a licensing pact for a “broad portfolio” of antibody-drug conjugates (ADCs) with several Hansoh subsidiaries, according to a stock exchange filing from Hansoh. The new deal encompasses two existing preclinical ADC programs, plus multiple programs based on Roche-proposed targets powered by Hansoh’s ADC platform technology.\nHansoh will be responsible for program development up to the completion of global Phase 1a trials, with Roche taking on further development outside of certain Asian territories, such as mainland China, Hong Kong, Macau and Taiwan.\nRoche is doling out $400 million upfront, plus a $110 million payment at the one-year mark. The rest of the massive potential total could come by way of milestone payments. Hansoh will also have the chance to collect tiered royalties.\nHansoh could also choose to contribute to ex-Asia development for two programs stemming from the alliance and gain incremental earnings from potential U.S. commercialization.\nThe two companies previously paired up almost exactly a year ago, with Roche paying $80 million upfront for certain licensing rights to HS-20110, a CDH17-targeting ADC using a topoisomerase inhibitor payload to treat solid tumors.\nObesity\nRoche expands obesity R&D footprint with Rona deal worth $1B+\nRoche has gained access to a genetic obesity approach from China’s Rona Therapeutics for up to $100 million in upfront and near-term payments.\nOctober 13, 2026\n·\n2 min read\n·\nMichael Gibney\nRead more\nNow Roche is back for more, building off a recent string of deals with other China companies. Just last week, the pharma gained access to a genetic obesity approach from Rona Therapeutics in a deal that could top $1 billion .\nRoche isn’t Hansoh’s only big pharma partner, even in the ADC space. At the end of 2023, U.K. pharma GSK paid $185 million upfront for access to Hansoh’s HS-20093—also known as risvutatug rezetecan (ris-rez)—in a deal that could surpass $1.5 billion.\nEarlier this summer, ris-rez hit the main goal of a pivotal study, extending overall survival for patients with small cell lung cancer. The findings represent the first positive Phase 3 overall survival data for a B7-H3–targeted ADC in any tumor type, GSK said at the time.\nIn early September, China regulators accepted for review Hansoh’s approval submission that is based on the Phase 3 data. Meanwhile, GSK intends to keep advancing the ADC across multiple tumor types.\nAntibody-drug conjugates\nGSK and Hansoh’s ADC clinches late-stage win, teeing up regulatory submission in China\nGSK and Hansoh Pharmaceutical’s antibody-drug conjugate success validates their partnership, one of the many deals in which Big Pharma has tapped a China company for promising cancer candidates.\nJuly 10, 2026\n·\n1 min read\n·\nGabrielle Masson\nRead more",
